sections.wikitext:
{{refimprove|section|date=March 2026}}
The "Surfin' U.S.A." single, backed with "Shut Down", was released under Capitol Records in the United States in March 1963. The song peaked on the ''Billboard'' pop chart at number three,<ref>{{Cite magazine |date=May 25, 1963 |title=Billboard Hot 100™️ |url=https://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/1963-05-25/ |access-date=May 1, 2024 |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]}}</ref> the band's first top ten hit therein (see also ''Surfin' Safari''). The B-side charted at number 23.<ref>{{Cite magazine |date=June 22, 1963 |title=Billboard Hot 100™️ |url=https://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/1963-06-22/ |access-date=May 1, 2024 |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]}}</ref> Although the double-sided hit single registered in ''Billboard'' as number one in chart points at the end of the year<ref>{{Citation |title=Surfin` U.S.A. - The Beach Boys |url=https://rockalia.com/es/https%3A%2F%2Frockalia.com%2Fes%2F |access-date=2026-07-08 |language=es-es}}</ref> (tabulated up to mid November 1963) and was cited by ''Billboard'' as "best-selling record of the year"<ref>{{Cite web |last=McKay |first=Charles |date=2019-08-08 |title=The Beach Boys: Surfin' U.S.A. - Song of the Day |url=https://nyfos.org/the-beach-boys-surfin-u-s-a/ |access-date=2026-07-08 |website=NYFOS |language=en-US}}</ref>, in a low-selling year for singles in the US it apparently did not initially sell a million copies until it was certified double Platinum in 2026. The song was re-issued in the U.S. as a single in July 1974 backed with "[[The Warmth of the Sun]]". That single also hit the ''Billboard'' Hot 100 chart, peaking at #3 in May 25, 1963.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/1963-05-25/ |title=Billboard Hot 100™ – Week of May 25, 1963 |publisher=Billboard |access-date=September 1, 2026}}</ref>

Writing in 2014, music historian Luis Sanchez highlighted the "Surfin' U.S.A." single as a turning point for the band, "creat[ing] a direct passage to California life for a wide teenage audience&nbsp;... [and] a distinct Southern California sensibility that exceeded its conception as such to advance right to the front of American consciousness".<ref>{{cite book |last=Sanchez |first=Luis |title=The Beach Boys' Smile |url={{google books |plainurl=y |id=FC0_AwAAQBAJ}} |year=2014 |publisher=Bloomsbury Publishing |isbn=978-1-62356-956-3|pp=10, 13}}</ref> ''[[Cash Box]]'' described it as "a pounding '[[Sweet Little Sixteen]]'-flavored rocker...that the Beach Boys belt out with coin-catching enthusiasm" and added that it is a "terrific instrumental showcase."<ref name=cb>{{cite magazine |title=CashBox Record Reviews |date=March 9, 1963 |page=48 |access-date=January 12, 2022 |url=https://worldradiohistory.com/Archive-All-Music/Cash-Box/60s/1963/CB-1963-03-09.pdf |magazine=Cash Box}}</ref>

In the United Kingdom, the single was released in June 1963. The third single by the band to be issued in the UK, it became the first single to chart. It peaked at 34 (28 in the ''[[New Musical Express]]''). In Australia, the single was released in 1963 and peaked at 9, becoming the band's first single to chart in Australia. The single was re-released in Australia in 1974 and again charted, peaking at 66. In Canada and Sweden, the single was released in 1963 and peaked on the charts at 6 in both countries. In July 1963, in the national charts used by ''Billboard'', it peaked at #9 in Hong Kong, #8 in Austria the following month; in August 1964 at #9 for two weeks in Japan.{{cn|date=March 2026}}

